:: Talk to Clippy 2
"How can I assist your investigation, Detective?" Clippy asks, bending into the shape of an interrogation lamp.

<button class="accordion" type="button">Ask about Sir Regex</button>
<div class="panel">
	<p>"Sir Regex was a man of&hellip; standards. He believed in strict validation for everything, from file formats to family members. He was recently working on a major update to his personal schemas. It seems he found an invalid entry."</p>
</div>

<button class="accordion" type="button">Ask about the other residents</button>
<div class="panel">
	<p>"A fascinating collection of data types, aren't they? It looks like you're trying to build a list of suspects. Some are more compressed than others. Some loop endlessly. And some&hellip; well, some aren't what they appear to be at all. Would you like me to format that as a bulleted list?"</p>
</div>

<button class="accordion" type="button">Ask about the murder weapon</button>
<div class="panel">
	<p>"The weapon? Ah, a classic query. It appears the cause of deletion was not a physical object but a corruption of the primary directory. A fatal exception, if you will." He briefly flickers into the shape of a knife, a rope, and a poisoned teacup.</p>
</div>

<button class="accordion" type="button">Ask who tidied the foyer</button>
<div class="panel">
	<p>The paperclip goes very still. "I did, Detective. At a quarter past eight." A pause of precisely one frame. "Before the body was found. I am told that is unusual. I find it efficient."</p>
</div>

<div class="start-menu">
	<button class="button" data-passage="Look at the map (Day 1)">Back to the map</button>
</div>


:: Explore the Library [Clue]
You have walked into the Library. On your way in you notice a waste bin, and a crumpled piece of paper beside it that you pick up. Most of it has been scribbled through with the same fountain pen, twice, hard enough to tear. Only a fragment of a PRONOM identifier survives: <code>x-fmt/&hellip;</code>

Someone did not want a format identification to be read.

Then you notice a dim glow on a desk. The archival PC is still logged in, the PRONOM registry open in the browser. Beside the keyboard, open at the last page he wrote, is a hand-ruled ledger titled <em>HOUSEHOLD &mdash; SIGNATURES TAKEN</em>.

<div class="map-figure">
	<img src="Images/The_Library_Computer.png" alt="An archival PC left logged in, the PRONOM registry open on screen">
	<a class="hotspot" style="left: 17%; top: 29%; width: 30%; height: 26%;" href="https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/PRONOM/BasicSearch/proBasicSearch.aspx?status=new" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" aria-label="Search the PRONOM registry in a new tab"></a>
</div>
<p class="caption">Click the screen to search PRONOM in a new tab. The registry is real; so are the signatures in the ledger.</p>

<div class="start-menu">
	<button class="button button--primary" data-passage="Library Puzzle">Read the signature ledger</button>
	<button class="button" data-passage="Look at the map (Day 1)">Back to the map</button>
</div>


:: Library Puzzle [Puzzle]
<div class="puzzle-brief">
<strong>Sir Regex's ledger.</strong> Five specimens, each recorded as the first bytes of a file and nothing else &mdash; no filename, no extension, no claim you could take on trust. He has left the format column blank. Fill it in.
<span class="brief-sep"></span>
Two of these specimens begin with the same four bytes. Four bytes are not an identification.
</div>

<div class="puzzle" id="lib-puzzle">

<div class="specimen-row">
	<div class="specimen"><code>49 49 2A 00 08 00 00 00</code><small>Specimen A &mdash; loose plate, estate inventory</small></div>
	<div><select id="libA" aria-label="Format of specimen A">
		<option value="">&mdash; identify &mdash;</option>
		<option value="tiff">TIFF</option>
		<option value="jpeg">JPEG</option>
		<option value="png">PNG</option>
		<option value="gif">GIF</option>
		<option value="pdf">PDF</option>
		<option value="zip">ZIP (or a ZIP-based container)</option>
		<option value="wav">WAV</option>
		<option value="webp">WebP</option>
	</select></div>
</div>

<div class="specimen-row">
	<div class="specimen"><code>52 49 46 46 24 08 00 00 57 41 56 45</code><small>Specimen B &mdash; dictation cylinder, transferred 1994</small></div>
	<div><select id="libB" aria-label="Format of specimen B">
		<option value="">&mdash; identify &mdash;</option>
		<option value="tiff">TIFF</option>
		<option value="jpeg">JPEG</option>
		<option value="png">PNG</option>
		<option value="gif">GIF</option>
		<option value="pdf">PDF</option>
		<option value="zip">ZIP (or a ZIP-based container)</option>
		<option value="wav">WAV</option>
		<option value="webp">WebP</option>
	</select></div>
</div>

<div class="specimen-row">
	<div class="specimen"><code>52 49 46 46 90 2C 00 00 57 45 42 50</code><small>Specimen C &mdash; "the portrait in the tea room"</small></div>
	<div><select id="libC" aria-label="Format of specimen C">
		<option value="">&mdash; identify &mdash;</option>
		<option value="tiff">TIFF</option>
		<option value="jpeg">JPEG</option>
		<option value="png">PNG</option>
		<option value="gif">GIF</option>
		<option value="pdf">PDF</option>
		<option value="zip">ZIP (or a ZIP-based container)</option>
		<option value="wav">WAV</option>
		<option value="webp">WebP</option>
	</select></div>
</div>

<div class="specimen-row">
	<div class="specimen"><code>50 4B 03 04 14 00 06 00</code><small>Specimen D &mdash; solicitor's correspondence bundle</small></div>
	<div><select id="libD" aria-label="Format of specimen D">
		<option value="">&mdash; identify &mdash;</option>
		<option value="tiff">TIFF</option>
		<option value="jpeg">JPEG</option>
		<option value="png">PNG</option>
		<option value="gif">GIF</option>
		<option value="pdf">PDF</option>
		<option value="zip">ZIP (or a ZIP-based container)</option>
		<option value="wav">WAV</option>
		<option value="webp">WebP</option>
	</select></div>
</div>

<div class="specimen-row">
	<div class="specimen"><code>89 50 4E 47 0D 0A 1A 0A</code><small>Specimen E &mdash; parish register, digitised</small></div>
	<div><select id="libE" aria-label="Format of specimen E">
		<option value="">&mdash; identify &mdash;</option>
		<option value="tiff">TIFF</option>
		<option value="jpeg">JPEG</option>
		<option value="png">PNG</option>
		<option value="gif">GIF</option>
		<option value="pdf">PDF</option>
		<option value="zip">ZIP (or a ZIP-based container)</option>
		<option value="wav">WAV</option>
		<option value="webp">WebP</option>
	</select></div>
</div>

</div>

<div class="start-menu">
	<<link "Complete the ledger">>
		<<set _try to setup.bump("library")>>
		<<set _right to setup.score({ libA: "tiff", libB: "wav", libC: "webp", libD: "zip", libE: "png" })>>
		<<if _right is 5>>
			<<replace "#lib-puzzle">>
				<div class="verdict verdict--ok">
					<h2>Ledger complete</h2>
					<p>Five signatures, five formats. The two <code>52 49 46 46</code> rows are both RIFF containers &mdash; and RIFF tells you the box, never the contents. Specimen B declares <code>WAVE</code> at offset 8; specimen C declares <code>WEBP</code>.</p>
					<hr>
					<p>Sir Regex had got there before you. Below specimen E, in a hand that has begun to shake, there is a sixth row he did not finish:</p>
					<div class="hexdump"><span class="offset">SPECIMEN F</span>  <span class="hl">52 49 46 46</span> ?? ?? ?? ?? ?? ?? ?? ??
<span class="offset">SOURCE    </span>  certificate of authenticity, hung in the tea room
<span class="offset">FORMAT    </span>  <span class="hl">RIFF &mdash; but of what? MUST OBTAIN THE FORM TYPE.</span></div>
					<p>He was four bytes into identifying a member of his own household, and he knew four bytes were not enough. He needed offset 8. He did not get it.</p>
					<p><em>You are carrying a Portable Integrity Scanner. It reads offset 8.</em></p>
				</div>
				<<logged "signature_ledger">>
				<div class="start-menu">
					<button class="button" data-passage="Look at the map (Day 1)">Back to the map</button>
				</div>
			<</replace>>
		<<elseif _try < 2>>
			<<replace "#lib-puzzle">>
				<div class="verdict verdict--warn">
					<h2>Incomplete identification</h2>
					<p><<= _right>> of the 5 rows agree with the registry. The ledger is pencil; you may rule the wrong ones out and write again.</p>
					<p>Two specimens open with the same four bytes. Whatever you wrote for those two, at least one of them cannot be right on four bytes alone &mdash; look at what the file declares <em>after</em> them.</p>
					<<attemptnote "library">>
				</div>
				<div class="start-menu">
					<button class="button button--primary" data-passage="Library Puzzle">Work through the ledger again</button>
				</div>
			<</replace>>
		<<else>>
			<<replace "#lib-puzzle">>
				<div class="comic-sans-letter">
					<h2>Helpfully filed</h2>
					<p>You write in the last row. A cheerful chime sounds from the stacks.</p>
					<p>"It looks like you're trying to identify a file format!" Clippy says, lifting the ledger neatly out from under your pen. "Misidentification can be so misleading. I'll have this catalogued properly."</p>
					<p>He is gone before you can object, and so is the ledger. You did not see the sixth row.</p>
				</div>
				<div class="start-menu">
					<button class="button" data-passage="Look at the map (Day 1)">Back to the map</button>
				</div>
			<</replace>>
		<</if>>
	<</link>>
	<button class="button button--quiet" data-passage="Explore the Library">Put the ledger down</button>
</div>


